  "_review_units": "The granularity at which review recency is tracked. Defaults to one level below src/, app/, lib/, server/, api/, packages/, apps/ — roughly 'the auth code', 'the billing code'. Per-file would be unreadable on any real repository; per-repository would be useless.",
  "review_units": null,

  "_gate": "ADVISORY by default: findings are reported and the build is not this script's call. The registry is human-editable and every field in it is written by the party the gate would be checking, so validating a self-assertion is not binding it — two independent reviews reached that conclusion. This tool exists to shrink the surface a human reviews and to keep closed problems closed, not to replace the human. Set mode to \"blocking\" where your process makes the registry trustworthy (protected branch, required reviewers on security/) — that is a decision about your controls, not about this script. One exception blocks in either mode: a staged credential of a known format, because a key that reaches a remote is rotated, not undone. block_at_severity is the lowest severity that counts: Critical, High, Medium, or Low.",
  "gate": {
    "mode": "advisory",
    "block_at_severity": "High",
    "block_on_never_reviewed": false,
    "stale_at_commits": 20
  },

  "_declared_stores": "Caches, buckets, search indexes, queues, log sinks, and third-party processors — the places personal data lands that no static scan finds reliably, and exactly where deletion quietly fails to reach (PII-05). Declaring one puts it in front of the reviewer. An empty list is reported as undeclared, never read as 'there are none'.",
  "declared_stores": [],

